# Self-Review Checklist (Pre-Push Gate)

> "If your reviewer has to guess something, your PR is not ready."

Before pushing to remote or opening a PR, **every item must be verified**.
Run `just review` to automate the technical checks.

## A. Functional Cohesion

- [ ] PR addresses a **single objective** (one feature / one fix / one refactor)
- [ ] Expected behavior is documented in commit messages or PR description
- [ ] No unintended side effects on other pipelines or models
- [ ] Edge cases are handled (NULL values, empty datasets, API failures)

## B. Diff Hygiene

- [ ] No unrelated files modified (formatting-only changes, unneeded config edits)
- [ ] No commented-out code left behind
- [ ] No `TODO` / `FIXME` without a linked issue
- [ ] No temporary debug code (`print()`, hardcoded test values)
- [ ] Commits follow layering order (see `git-workflow.mdc`)

## C. Security & Secrets

- [ ] No credentials, API keys, or passwords in the diff
- [ ] No `.env` file committed (only `.env.example`)
- [ ] No hardcoded connection strings (use Airflow Connections/Variables)
- [ ] Sensitive files are in `.gitignore`

## D. Code Quality (Python)

- [ ] `just check` passes (ruff lint + format)
- [ ] No `any` type annotations (TypeScript) or untyped functions
- [ ] Naming is explicit — no `data`, `tmp`, `x`, `df2`
- [ ] No unnecessary complexity (KISS principle)
- [ ] No duplication (DRY) — extract to shared utilities if repeated 3+ times
- [ ] Code readable without oral explanation

## E. SQL / dbt Models

- [ ] No `SELECT *` — all columns explicitly named
- [ ] JOIN conditions verified (no accidental cartesian products)
- [ ] NULL handling explicit (`COALESCE`, `NULLIF`, or documented)
- [ ] Aggregations correct (GROUP BY matches SELECT)
- [ ] `ref()` and `source()` used correctly (no hardcoded table names)
- [ ] No dependency cycles introduced (check `dbt ls --select +model_name+`)
- [ ] Schema tests defined in `_models.yml` (`not_null`, `unique`, `accepted_values`)
- [ ] `+database: pg` set for core/mart models (not DuckDB in-memory)

## F. Airflow DAGs

- [ ] DAG id is stable and descriptive
- [ ] No heavy logic in DAG file (orchestration only, processing in Docker)
- [ ] Task dependencies are explicit (`>>` or `chain()`)
- [ ] `retries`, `retry_delay`, `execution_timeout` set in `default_args`
- [ ] `max_active_runs=1` for data pipelines
- [ ] `catchup=False` unless backfill intended
- [ ] No `Variable.get()` at module level (parse-time penalty)
- [ ] Asset emissions (`outlets`) match the event chain
- [ ] Pool assignments correct (`docker_pool`, `database_pool`, `api_pool`)

## G. Data Correctness (Data Engineering Critical)

- [ ] Transformations produce expected business results
- [ ] Verified on sample data (row counts, sums, distributions)
- [ ] Before/after comparison if modifying existing models
- [ ] Idempotent — pipeline can be re-run without side effects
- [ ] Incremental logic handles late-arriving data correctly

## H. Docker & Infrastructure

- [ ] Docker images build successfully if Dockerfile changed (`just build-all`)
- [ ] Containers start and pass health checks
- [ ] No `:latest` tags — all images pinned to versions
- [ ] `mem_limit` set for DockerOperator tasks
- [ ] Entrypoint scripts tested after modification
- [ ] Bind mounts don't overwrite `.venv` or entrypoint

## I. Tests & Quality Gates

- [ ] `just test` passes (unit tests)
- [ ] New code has corresponding tests
- [ ] dbt tests pass if models changed (`just dbt-test`)
- [ ] Quality checks (GX/Soda) target the correct schema and tables
- [ ] No broken or flaky tests ignored

## J. Documentation & Observability

- [ ] `docs/pipeline.md` or `docs/lakehouse.md` updated if architecture changed
- [ ] dbt model descriptions updated in `_models.yml`
- [ ] DAG `doc_md` set for new/modified DAGs
- [ ] Logs are meaningful (not verbose noise)
- [ ] Alerting configured if the change is critical path

## K. Final Gate (Human Judgment)

- [ ] I can explain this PR in under 2 minutes without looking at the code
- [ ] I understand exactly what breaks if this fails
- [ ] A reviewer can understand the PR by reading commits in order
- [ ] I tested on realistic data, not just empty/trivial cases
